0174 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050174 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050174 #edb/ref 1031 "Reasoning With Equivalent Ratios" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050174 #edb/ref 1033 563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050174 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "33fcd530-1cfd-5f42-a911-762522fe7130" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1031 "Writing Ratios Using Fractions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1033 552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1034 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050481 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "4d30ea10-ea6d-511a-a7e5-cce5fffa2a64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050709 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050709 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050709 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050709 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186208826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050709 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186208827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050709 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186208828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050709 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186208829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050709 #edb/ref 1031 "Ratios" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050709 #edb/ref 1033 6056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050709 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "607ed358-6a5e-5a58-b89a-0334343a1121" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073563 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2277" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073563 #edb/ref 1071 "In Jennifer's garden, there are $60$ roses. The ratio of roses to orchids is $\\dfrac{3}{5}$. How many orchids are there in the garden?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073563 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "913c1995-fe26-5359-8298-e3b8379e39d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073563 #edb/ref 1074 "The ratio of roses to orchids is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of roses and the denominator represents the number of orchids.\n\nWe can make a numerator of $60$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $20$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}\\times 20}{{\\color{red}5}\\times 20} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}60}}{{\\color{red}100}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in Jennifer's garden, there are $100$ orchids." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073564 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2278" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073564 #edb/ref 1071 "The ratio of sand to crushed stone in concrete is $\\dfrac{3}{2}$. If Daniel has $80$ kilograms of crushed stone available to make concrete, what amount of sand should he use?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073564 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2eeba279-cd91-55c7-b423-a14edb255ebb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073564 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given that the ratio of sand to crushed stone in concrete is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the amount of sand and the denominator represents the amount of crushed stone.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $80$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $40$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}\\times 40}{{\\color{red}2}\\times 40} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}120}}{{\\color{red}80}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $80$ kilograms of crushed stone, Daniel must use $120$ kilograms of sand." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073881 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073881 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073881 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073881 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073881 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073881 #edb/ref 1039 "Using a Ratio to Compute a Denominator" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073881 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073881 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "48f71a07-2bd3-5164-a227-b82d56c0abc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073882 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073882 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073882 #edb/ref 1039 "Using a Ratio to Compute a Numerator" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073882 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073882 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d710628f-14e0-5587-90d1-564a5e0ead19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073981 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073981 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073981 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073981 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073981 #edb/ref 1045 "Reasoning With Equivalent Ratios" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073981 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186074874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073981 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073981 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ec7b59c9-abe8-57a3-a286-1bbf406859b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074874 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074874 #edb/ref 1061 7792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074874 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1f40eb50-1f7f-5b78-a04c-ac11239e4005" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074874 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186074967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074875 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074875 #edb/ref 1061 7793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074875 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b00d4672-3ee3-5870-9ffd-2a7b3538ab54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074875 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074876 #edb/ref 1061 7794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074876 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5144de57-8e74-52bb-a605-38d854105198" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074876 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074967 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, we are given a ratio and want to find an amount. In these cases, it's helpful to use equivalent fractions.\n\nFor example, if the ratio of cows to sheep on Margaret's farm is $11:7$, and she has $22$ cows, how many sheep are there on the farm?\n\nTo solve this, we start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}11}:{\\color{red}7}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}11}{\\color{red}7}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of cows and the denominator represents the number of sheep.\n\nSince there are $22$ cows on the farm, we would like a numerator of $22$. We make this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}11}\\times 2}{{\\color{red}7}\\times 2} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}22}}{{\\color{red}14}}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is now the number of sheep on the farm if there are $22$ cows. Therefore, on Margaret's farm, there are $\\color{red}14$ sheep." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074967 #edb/ref 1065 2050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074967 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cfd89526-b68e-5436-a4e1-e20aa477ab68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074967 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
